      <p>
      Use the buttons to slice the surface <m>\frac{x^2}{4} + \frac{y^2}{9} + \frac{z^2}{16} = 1</m> with planes
      <m>x=c</m>, <m>y=c</m>, or <m>z=c</m>, and drag the slider to vary <m>c</m>.
      The equation of each cross-section is displayed above the figure.
      Drag the figure to view it from a different angle, or press the
      <q>Rotate</q> button to spin it automatically.</p>
    </instructions>
  </interactive>
 </figure>

 <p>
 The animation in <xref ref="fig-ellipsoid-video"/> shows these slices being taken one at a time.
 </p>

 <figure xml:id="fig-ellipsoid-video">
  <caption>
    Slicing <m>\frac{x^2}{4} + \frac{y^2}{9} + \frac{z^2}{16} = 1</m> with planes <m>z = c</m>,
    <m>x = c</m>, and <m>y = c</m>.
  </caption>
  <video xml:id="video-ellipsoid-slices"
         source="videos/ellipsoid_slices_manim.mp4"
         width="100%" aspect="16:9"/>
 </figure>

 <p>
 
 </p>



</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-elliptic-cone">
  <title>Elliptic Cone</title>

<p>
    We now consider the case where from equation <xref ref="quad-simplified"/> where
    <me>
    A = 1/4, B= 1/9, C= -1, D= 0, E= 0
    </me>  
    Under these conditions, the general equation of a quadric surface reduces to the following equation:
    <me>
        z^2 = \frac{x^2}{4} + \frac{y^2}{9} 

    </me>
  </p>

<p>
  Note that this looks similar to the equation of an elliptical paraboloid <xref ref="subsec-elliptical-paraboloid"/>
  but <m>z</m> has the same degree as <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.  Consequently, since <m>z</m> has the same degree
  as <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, the traces would behave linearly instead of quadratically.  Structurally, from the traces,
  two hyperbolas and an ellipse.  We can see the traces through the intersection of the planes 
  <m>x = 2</m>, <m>y = -3</m>, and <m>z = 1</m>, as shown in <xref ref="fig-elliptic-cone-slices"/>:

  </p>
    
<md>
    <mrow>z^2 - \frac{y^2}{9} \amp= 1  \amp\amp (hyperbola)</mrow>
    <mrow>z^2 - \frac{x^2}{4} \amp= 1  \amp\amp (hyperbola)</mrow>
    <mrow>\frac{x^2}{4} + \frac{y^2}{9} \amp= 1 \amp\amp (ellipse)</mrow>
</md>

 <figure xml:id="fig-elliptic-cone-traces">
